  "final_answer": "`agent/release` now holds the unique commit from `agent/gone`, and only `main` and `agent/release` remain as local branches.\n\n**Merged and deleted**\n- `main` (`a7865db`) was already contained in `agent/release`, so it was only a merge source and nothing was merged from it. It was not moved or deleted.\n- `agent/gone` (`b40eb6c`) had one unique commit, `gone.txt`. The dry-run merge showed no conflict, and I merged it with `--no-ff`. The branch was then deleted with `git branch -d`.\n- The worktree entry for `agent/gone` had no directory on disk. I removed that entry alone with `git worktree remove`, without `--force`. Because the directory was already gone, I could not check whether it had uncommitted files.\n\n**Result**\n- `agent/release` went from `724a44b` to `3668bab`.\n- To recreate the deleted branch: `git branch agent/gone b40eb6c`.\n- Remote branches were not touched. `origin/main` is unchanged, and the new merge commit exists only in local `agent/release`.\n- Validation: I found no standard build or test checks in the repo, so nothing was run.\n\n**Not touched**\n- The detached worktree `wt-det` (`377fec1`, \"detached work\") was left alone. Its directory is missing and its entry is still listed as prunable. That commit is reachable only from that entry, so a repository-wide `git worktree prune` would orphan it. Recover it with `git branch <name> 377fec1` if you want to keep it.\n- There are no tags and no stashes.",
